			<div class="content" id="post-content-4858358">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>As the energy carried by light will magnify with the shortening of wave length, energy coming from nowhere accumulates in the universe and finally caused… deviation of balance of material and energy. Then unexpected results, like the worst - affect the gradual cosmic expansion or bring near the final collapse.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This feels&#8230;like a lot of escalation? Like I get why there's ever growing amounts of energy in the system cos the effects of this changing the wavelength and increasing the energy of light; but it feels&#8230;like the universe is so large that this effect would be drowned out by the sheer size of the universe long before it became an actual issue; particularly since you can e.g. put it deep underground with minimal light in the style of a <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neutrino_detector" onclick="window.open(this.href, '_blank'); return false;">Neutrino Detector</a>.</p>
<p>Even ignoring this, how are you showing this escalation within the article? What do you like, intend to do to convey the threat and have folk try to like, prevent it happening and like, actually get a reader invested in this escalation?</p>
<p>The name 'spectroscope' is probably fine; it's very much the kinda thing you're going to have to describe by like, explaining what it does regardless of name so *shrugs*</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Not very sure how to merge the final world-threatening narrative into this ordinary object-describing document. Will a research report (regarding the status of the universe) or a test log work well?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This question raises a lot of concern in me. See, the answer very much depends on what the actual world-threatening narrative is and how you want to tell it: perhaps a series of updates over thousands of years would work if it's slow (see e.g. <a href="/scp-4792">SCP-4792</a> for an example of this kind of thing), or some journal entries from a researcher attached to it if you want a quick thing, or some mix of the two. But at it's core, it really just depends on how the story like&#8230;unfolds?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

							</div>

								
					
